sonal\">\nL172:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Yun-Nung</namePart>\nL173:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Chen</namePart>\nL174:                 <role>\nL175:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL176:                 </role>\nL177:             </name>\nL178:             <originInfo>\nL179:                 <publisher>Association for Computational Linguistics</publisher>\nL180:                 <place>\nL181:                     <placeTerm type=\"text\">Miami, Florida, USA</placeTerm>\nL182:                 </place>\nL183:             </originInfo>\nL184:             <genre authority=\"marcgt\">conference publication</genre>\nL185:         </relatedItem>\nL186:         <abstract>In this work, we are interested in automated methods for knowledge graph creation (KGC) from input text. Progress on large language models (LLMs) has prompted a series of recent works applying them to KGC, e.g., via zero/few-shot prompting. Despite successes on small domain-specific datasets, these models face difficulties scaling up to text common in many real-world applications.\nL187: A principal issue is that, in prior methods, the KG schema has to be included in the LLM prompt to generate valid triplets; larger and more complex schemas easily exceed the LLMs’ context window length. Furthermore, there are scenarios where a fixed pre-defined schema is not available and we would like the method to construct a high-quality KG with a succinct self-generated schema.\nL188: To address these problems, we propose a three-phase framework named Extract-Define-Canonicalize (EDC): open information extraction followed by schema definition and post-hoc canonicalization. EDC is flexible in that it can be applied to settings where a pre-defined target schema is available and when it is not; in the latter case, it constructs a schema automatically and applies self-canonicalization.\nL189: To further improve performance, we introduce a trained component that retrieves schema elements relevant to the input text; this improves the LLMs’ extraction performance in a retrieval-augmented generation-like manner. We demonstrate on three KGC benchmarks that EDC is able to extract high-quality triplets without any parameter tuning and with significantly larger schemas compared to prior works. Code for EDC is available at https://github.com/clear-nus/edc.</abstract>\nL190:         <identifier type=\"citekey\">zhang-soh-2024-extract</identifier>\nL191:         <identifier type=\"doi\">10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.548</identifier>\nL192:         <location>\nL193:             <url>https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.548/</url>\nL194:         </location>\nL195:         <part>\nL196:             <date>2024-11</date>\nL197:             <extent unit=\"page\">\nL198:                 <start>9820</start>\nL199:                 <end>9836</end>\nL200:             </extent>\nL201:         </part>\nL202:     </mods>\nL203:     </modsCollection>\nL204: Download as File\nL205: \nL206:     %0 Conference Proceedings\nL207:     %T Extract, Define, Canonicalize: An LLM-based Framework for Knowledge Graph Construction\nL208:     %A Zhang, Bowen\nL209:     %A Soh, Harold\nL210:     %Y Al-Onaizan, Yaser\nL211:     %Y Bansal, Mohit\nL212:     %Y Chen, Yun-Nung\nL213:     %S Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing\nL214:     %D 2024\nL215:     %8 November\nL216:     %I Association for Computational Linguistics\nL217:     %C Miami, Florida, USA\nL218:     %F zhang-soh-2024-extract\nL219:     %X In this work, we are interested in automated methods for knowledge graph creation (KGC) from input text. Progress on large language models (LLMs) has prompted a series of recent works applying them to KGC, e.g., via zero/few-shot prompting. Despite successes on small domain-specific datasets, these models face difficulties scaling up to text common in many real-world applications.\nL220: A principal issue is that, in prior methods, the KG schema has to be included in the LLM prompt to generate valid triplets; larger and more complex schemas easily exceed the LLMs’ context window length. Furthermore, there are scenarios where a fixed pre-defined schema is not available and we would like the method to construct a high-quality KG with a succinct self-generated schema.\nL221: To address these problems, we propose a three-phase framework named Extract-Define-Canonicalize (EDC): open information extraction followed by schema definition and post-hoc canonicalization. EDC is flexible in that it can be applied to settings where a pre-defined target schema is available and when it is not; in the latter case, it constructs a schema automatically and applies self-canonicalization.\nL222: To further improve performance, we introduce a trained component that retrieves schema elements relevant to the input text; this improves the LLMs’ extraction performance in a retrieval-augmented generation-like manner. We demonstrate on three KGC benchmarks that EDC is able to extract high-quality triplets without any parameter tuning and with significantly larger schemas compared to prior works. Code for EDC is available at https://github.com/clear-nus/edc.\nL223:     %R 10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.548\nL224:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.548/\nL225:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.548\nL226:     %P 9820-9836\nL227: Download as File\nL228: ##### Markdown (Informal)\nL229: \nL230: [Extract, Define, Canonicalize: An LLM-based Framework for Knowledge Graph Construction](https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.548/) (Zhang & Soh, EMNLP 2024)\nL231: \nL232:   * cite32†Extract, Define, Canonicalize: An LLM-based Framework for Knowledge Graph Construction (Zhang & Soh, EMNLP 2024)\nL233: ##### ACL\nL234: \nL235:   * Bowen Zhang and Harold Soh. 2024. cite32†Extract, Define, Canonicalize: An LLM-based Framework for Knowledge Graph Construction . 